\section{Treat a Slot as an ABI Contract}

In the toy ABI used here, a class in a single inheritance
chain begins with a pointer to its table. A subclass
inherits the parent's field offsets and table slots. A
new virtual declaration reserves a new slot; an override
replaces the target of an existing slot. The entry has
the signature promised to callers of the original method.
These choices make a call compiled through a base type
work on an object allocated by a subclass, including when
the caller was compiled before the subclass existed.

The compiler design handbook's chapter on object-oriented
optimization illustrates this layout for single inheritance
and uses embedded base objects to explain why a more
complex layout is needed for multiple inheritance. Those
ideas are not a specification of all C++ targets. The
toy offsets below are chosen for exposition; real targets
also specify alignment, metadata, and construction rules.

\section{Keep Both the Object Address and the View Address in Mind}

A \texttt{Dial} contains a \texttt{Widget} view at byte
0 and a \texttt{Colored} view at byte 24. A routine
accepting \texttt{Colored} receives a pointer into the
\texttt{Dial}; it does not need to know that \texttt{Dial}
exists. That pointer names the beginning of the
\texttt{Colored} subobject, where its table pointer lives.
Now \texttt{Dial} overrides \texttt{tint} and implements it
with access to fields in the full object. The selected
entry expects the view address but the overriding body
expects the complete address. An entry thunk adapts one
to the other. \Cref{fig:visual-405} shows this with
addresses relative to the complete object.

