Slot 0 in one view is unrelated to slot 0 in the other.
An inherited body such as \texttt{Colored.mix} must not
be adjusted to the complete object simply because the
complete object has type \texttt{Dial}. The adjustment
depends on the entry's incoming view and the selected
body's expected view. The D specification describes a
related mechanism for interface views and adjustor
thunks; its concrete representation is its own ABI.

% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Lay Out Entries With the Correct Incoming Receiver}

\begin{verbatim}
build_table(complete, view):
    entries := []
    for family in view.slot_order:
        body := select_override(complete, view, family)
        require_compatible_override(body, family.contract)
        from_offset := offset_of_view(complete, view)
        to_offset := offset_of_view(complete, body.expects_view)
        delta := to_offset - from_offset
        entries.append(make_entry(body, delta, family.contract))
    return entries

make_entry(body, delta, contract):
    if delta == 0 and same_abi(body, contract): return body
    return thunk(view_receiver, args):
        receiver := byte_offset(view_receiver, delta)
        result := call_body(body, receiver, adapt_args(contract, args))
        return adapt_result(body, contract, result)

upcast_to_colored(dial):
    if dial is null: return null
    return byte_offset(dial, 24)
\end{verbatim}

This toy code assumes statically determined offsets in the
table construction, concrete targets, and unambiguous
subobject paths. A real layout supporting virtual bases
may have to load an offset determined by the complete
class, and two occurrences of a repeated base may require
distinct paths even though the base name is identical.
An abstract slot needs a noncallable marker or a trap,
not accidental fallthrough to another slot. In any variant,
constructor code must set the appropriate table pointers
before making the views observable under the language's
construction semantics.

\section{Test Overrides, Results, and Layout Evolution}

For a concrete address trace, allocate a \texttt{Dial} at
1000. Upcasting to \texttt{Colored} yields 1024, whose
first slot points to the adjustor. The thunk computes
1000 and enters \texttt{Dial.tint}. Dispatching slot 1
instead passes 1024 unchanged. Use distinct sentinel values
in both subobjects so the test catches reading the wrong
field even when the call reaches the expected function.
Also test a null upcast; it must not manufacture an
interior address.

A covariant result can require adjustment in the other
direction. Suppose the slot promises to return a
\texttt{Colored} pointer and an override produces a
\texttt{Dial} pointer. The adapter must apply the required
upcast to that result, preserving null. A pure
receiver-adjusting entry might be a tail jump; an adapter
that has to transform the normal return generally needs
control after the body returns. Its unwind metadata must
describe that choice correctly.

For layout evolution, compile a caller against one version
and then link a class whose previous slots have been
reordered. The ABI checker must reject this mixture rather
than assuming a shared method spelling can save it.
Adding a base field can similarly shift a separately
compiled view. Version the ABI, force consumers to
recompile, or reserve a representation whose offset and
slot promises do not move. If an implementation has
moving collection, interior views also need a way to
identify their owning allocation or be represented as
base-plus-offset roots. A machine pointer to byte 24 is
not automatically a pointer to a separately allocated
object.

\begin{verbatim}
baseline: Colored.tint(1024) -> Dial.tint(1000)
baseline: Colored.mix(1024)  -> Colored.mix(1024)
new nonvirtual method: existing entries unchanged
reordered slot: old caller must not link under the same ABI
\end{verbatim}

Finally compare a direct call that takes a complete
\texttt{Dial} receiver with a virtual call through
\texttt{Colored}. The renderer should see the same
mutation in both cases. This is not a claim that the
addresses passed at the source call boundary match; it
demonstrates that the entry adapter makes the difference
irrelevant to the body.

\section{Keep Construction and Destruction Consistent With the View}

Assume this toy language suppresses derived virtual behavior
during a base initializer. Calling \texttt{tint} against the
\texttt{Colored} portion of a half-built \texttt{Dial} must
therefore select the base initializer's target, not a
\texttt{Dial} implementation that reads still-uninitialized
fields. One implementation installs construction-phase
tables when entering each base and installs the final tables
after the fields they require are ready. Another can restrict
which virtual calls are allowed during construction. Neither
policy follows from the byte offsets alone.

\begin{verbatim}
allocate Dial storage
install Colored construction table on view +24
initialize Colored fields
initialize Dial's own fields
install final tables for both views
publish Dial
\end{verbatim}

If construction fails after the base initializer, cleanup
must know which portions were actually constructed. Do not
route through a final-class destructor that assumes all
fields are live. If the language mirrors base-only behavior
on destruction, restore the corresponding phase view before
entering a base destructor. Test by letting a base initializer
and destructor each log a virtual call with a visible
implementation name, and then compare a call on a fully
constructed object. The trace should match the phase
specified by the language; memory layout inspection alone
cannot verify that rule.

For a moving collector, the table entry's transient interior
address and the object view stored in language variables may
have different liveness requirements. \Cref{ch:ext-408,ch:ext-411}
separates layout from the roots that must survive a
collection point.
In particular, a safepoint inside an adjustor that performs
a non-tail call has to describe any live managed receiver
under the representation used at that machine instruction.
